<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  OBD Platform - Phase C: the behaviour of a local SIP test endpoint.

  TEMPLATE - rendered at container start by infra/freeswitch-endpoint/scripts/entrypoint.sh.

  This is the entire "softphone application". When the platform switch calls
  this endpoint, the endpoint:

      1. ANSWERS                     -> the platform sees CHANNEL_ANSWER
      2. sends a KNOWN DTMF sequence -> the platform sees CHANNEL_DTMF, with a
                                         digit sequence this file chose, so the
                                         platform's DTMF handling can be checked
                                         against a known-good input
      3. plays a prompt              -> proves media flows endpoint -> platform
      4. WAITS                       -> stays answered and answered-back, so
                                         media flows platform -> endpoint too
      5. HANGS UP                    -> produces a real, voluntary hangup

  The order matters. DTMF is sent over RTP, and RTP only flows after the
  SDP has been negotiated and the channel answered, so the DTMF step must come
  after "answer" and it must not be the last thing that happens.

  MATCHING CONDITION
  Phase E corrected this. The original condition matched on
  ${sip_destination_user}, which is the user part of the REQUEST-URI. When the
  platform originates through a gateway, the Request-URI carries the GATEWAY's
  own identity rather than the number dialled - observed on this endpoint as:

      New Channel sofia/obd-endpoint/FreeSWITCH@endpoint-b:5060
      Processing FreeSWITCH <+15551230000>->1002 in context obd-endpoint
      No Route, Aborting

  The two values differ: the channel and Request-URI say FreeSWITCH, while
  destination_number is 1002. So ^\d+$ could never match a gateway-originated
  call, and the endpoint rejected a perfectly delivered INVITE with
  NO_ROUTE_DESTINATION. ${destination_number} is the number actually dialled and
  is what FreeSWITCH itself logs (->1002), so it is the correct thing to match.
  It stays deliberately narrow: a real deployment would not answer every call.

  WHY "wait" AND NOT "play_and_collect_digits"
  play_and_collect_digits would test the endpoint's DTMF RECEIVER, not its
  sender. Phase C needs the platform switch to be the receiver, so the endpoint
  must generate the tones. Step 2 does that with send_dtmf.
-->
<context name="obd-endpoint">
  <extension name="obd-endpoint-answer">
    <!--
      destination_number is the number actually dialled, and is what FreeSWITCH
      logs (->1002). The previous condition used sip_destination_user, which for
      a gateway-originated call is the GATEWAY's identity (FreeSWITCH), not the
      number dialled, so it could never match. See MATCHING CONDITION above.

      ONE condition, deliberately. An earlier attempt kept sip_destination_user
      as a nested fallback with break="false" on the outer condition. That was
      wrong: nested conditions are ANDed, so the extension still failed every
      gateway call (destination_number matched, sip_destination_user did not) and
      the endpoint rejected a perfectly delivered INVITE with NO_ROUTE_DESTINATION
      - the same symptom as before, which is what made the mistake expensive.
      Alternatives must be sibling extensions, or a single alternation
      expression. destination_number alone is sufficient: it is set on every
      inbound call, direct or gateway-originated.
    -->
    <condition field="${destination_number}" expression="^\d+$">
      <!-- 1. answer -->
      <action application="answer"/>

      <!-- 2. emit a known DTMF sequence toward the platform switch -->
      <action application="send_dtmf" data="@@DTMF_DIGITS@@"/>

      <!-- 3. send audio toward the platform switch -->
      <action application="playback" data="tone_stream://%(200,400,1000)"/>

      <!-- 4. stay answered so the platform switch can send media to us too -->
      <!--
        `sleep`, NOT `wait`. Phase E.1 measured that `wait` is NOT a registered
        application in this FreeSWITCH 1.11.1 build, while `sleep` (mod_dptools)
        is. The registry has wait_for_answer and wait_for_silence, but no `wait`.

        The failure mode was the expensive part. An unavailable application is
        not reported as an error to the caller and does not fail the call
        cleanly - FreeSWITCH aborts the channel:

            [ERR] switch_core_session.c:2771 Invalid Application wait
            [NOTICE] switch_core_session.c:2772 Hangup [CS_EXECUTE]
                                                    [DESTINATION_OUT_OF_ORDER]

        so the endpoint answered, ran send_dtmf and playback, then destroyed the
        channel roughly 600 ms after answer. On the platform that surfaced as
        commands against a channel that no longer existed:

            api uuid_broadcast <uuid> <file> aleg -> -ERR invalid uuid
            api uuid_kill <uuid> NORMAL_CLEARING  -> -ERR No such channel!
            api uuid_dump <uuid>                  -> sometimes succeeds

        which is what made this look like a uuid_broadcast or ESL command
        contract problem for several phases. It was a typo-level defect in this
        fixture that had been present since Phase C: the endpoint never actually
        held a call open, it only ever survived long enough to answer.
      -->
      <action application="sleep" data="@@ANSWER_HOLD_MS@@"/>

      <!-- 5. hang up voluntarily: NORMAL_CLEARING, not an error -->
      <action application="hangup"/>
    </condition>
  </extension>
</context>
